  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]` is placed on an explicitly declared function. The explanation is in fenced citation commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification.\n\nThe explanation is substantive and agrees with the source:\n- `Project` is a test fixture whose `node_content` and `source_node` map `OutlineData` to a `String` node and back. Its `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` ignore the node argument.\n- Its `outgoing_edges` returns an empty `Vec`. The only non-test caller of `Domain::node_path` that I found is `edge_fingerprint` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs`, which calls `node_path` once per target. With no targets it never calls it.\n- The `Project` flows retain `NodeId`s directly in dependencies and never compute a node path, so the commentary's claim that the required `Domain` method is unused there holds.\n- The commentary says the stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains. The `node_path` assertions I found use other domains: `ReviewDomain` in `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]`, and `TestDomain` in `agent/configured.rs`. I did not read the filesystem-storage tests themselves.\n\nThe reason is a specific one: a trait method the fixture must implement but never exercises. It is not an appeal to difficulty. The citation uses a permitted role and form, and the exception is limited to this function's zero execution.",
